                "location": "Kalimpong, West Bengal",
                "description": "<p>Kalimpong is a vibrant cultural crossroads where Himalayan, Tibetan, Lepcha, Nepali, and Bhutanese influences coexist. Located at an altitude of approximately 1,250 metres (4,101 ft), it is known for its pleasant climate, panoramic mountain views, and rich colonial heritage. Once an important trading post between India and Tibet, Kalimpong has evolved into a peaceful and culturally diverse destination.</p><h3>History & Heritage</h3><p>Kalimpong's history is deeply intertwined with Bhutanese and Tibetan rule before becoming a British hill station in the mid-19th century. The town became a vital hub for the wool trade with Tibet and a centre for Christian missionaries, who established some of the region's most prestigious schools. Today, colonial-era architecture, historic churches, and ancient Buddhist monasteries reflect its storied past.</p><h3>Geography & Climate</h3><p>Situated on a ridge connecting two hills, Deolo and Durpin, Kalimpong overlooks the Teesta River valley. The moderate altitude ensures a mild and pleasant climate throughout the year, unlike the colder temperatures of nearby Darjeeling. The region experiences heavy monsoon rains, which nourish its famous orchid nurseries and lush green landscapes.</p><h3>Culture & Lifestyle</h3><p>The town is a melting pot of cultures, home to indigenous Lepchas, ethnic Nepalis, Bhutias, and Tibetans. This diversity is celebrated through various festivals like Losar, Dashain, and Christmas. Kalimpong is also famous for its traditional handicrafts, including Tibetan thangkas, wood carvings, and local cheese (Kalimpong cheese).</p>",
